<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Need Pictures for the Classroom Newsletter.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Write responses to questions about informational texts.
reading_2: Create written reflections on stories read in class.
phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can respond to questions in a text about newsletter topics (e.g., 'When will this be?' 'What is your article about?' 'When did this happen?').
Can write reflections for the newsletter on short stories read in class (e.g., 'The story was funny.').
Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ɑ/ in 'art' and /oʊ/ in 'show').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures related to school events (e.g., a field trip, a sports day, a classroom project) on the board or screen. Students briefly discuss what they see. Teacher asks: "What do we need for our classroom newsletter?" (Elicit "pictures," "articles," "announcements").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a sample "Classroom News" headline and a short paragraph about a school event (e.g., "Our Fun Art Day! Yesterday, we made colorful posters. We need more pictures!"). The teacher reads the headline and paragraph aloud, pointing to the words. Teacher models identifying key words like "pictures," "art," "fun," and highlighting short and long vowel sounds in words like /ɑ/ in 'art' and /eɪ/ in 'made', /oʊ/ in 'show' (if applicable) and /ʌ/ in 'fun'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a book or a newsletter?" (A newsletter)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we need for the newsletter?" (Pictures)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text titled "Our Class Newsletter Update!" This text contains a brief announcement about upcoming articles and a request for pictures. It should include vocabulary: `article`, `newsletter`, `announcement`, `editor`, `class`, `pictures`, `event`, `topic`, `write`, `take`, `publish`, `exciting`, `new`. It also incorporates grammar: Present simple questions ("What else do we need?"), Future ("I will write the article."), and Past simple ("Last week, we started our new art project.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student is able to read short, simple informational texts and identify the main idea and specific details about familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Text Example:</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">"<b>Our Class Newsletter Update!</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Hello, everyone! This is an important announcement from your newsletter editor, Ms. Sofia. We are preparing a new and exciting edition of our classroom newsletter! We need pictures for the classroom newsletter articles. Juan will write an article about our exciting field trip to the zoo last month. What will you do for the newsletter? Maybe you can write about our new class pet? We also need photos of the students, and pictures from our amazing art class. Remember, a good newsletter needs colorful pictures! When will we publish it? Soon!</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Teacher asks: "What is this text mainly about?" (The classroom newsletter and needing pictures).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students read the text silently, then indicate True or False for statements like: "The newsletter editor is a student." (False) "Juan will write about a field trip." (True) "We need pictures for the articles." (True).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match the person to their role/action (e.g., Ms. Sofia – editor, Juan – write article about zoo trip).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific information, formulating and answering questions using Present Simple (questions), Past Simple (affirmative & interrogative), and Future (affirmative & interrogative). Identifying short and long vowel sounds in context. Target vocabulary: `article`, `newsletter`, `pictures`, `event`, `topic`, `write`, `take`, `publish`, `exciting`, `fun`, `teacher`, `student`, `school`.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentences from the "Our Class Newsletter Update!" text with key words (from the target vocabulary) missing. Students read the sentences and fill in the blanks using words from a provided word bank.
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Example: "We are preparing a new and exciting edition of our classroom _________." (newsletter)</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">"We need ________ for the classroom newsletter articles." (pictures)</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Teacher also includes a short phonemic awareness exercise where students identify if a word from the text has a short or long vowel sound (e.g., "Is the 'a' in 'class' short or long?" /æ/ short; "Is the 'i' in 'write' short or long?" /aɪ/ long).</p>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Question and Answer Generation:</b> Students re-read the text individually. The teacher provides sentence starters or specific facts from the text. Students must write a question and then answer it based on the text.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Fact: "Juan will write an article about our exciting field trip." → Question: "What will Juan write about?" Answer: "He will write about the field trip." (Future tense)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Fact: "Last month, we went to the zoo." → Question: "When did they go to the zoo?" Answer: "They went to the zoo last month." (Past simple)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Fact: "The newsletter needs photos." → Question: "What does the newsletter need?" Answer: "It needs photos." (Present simple)</li>
                    </ul>
                    Teacher circulates, checking for accuracy in question formation and answers, and prompting for correct pronunciation of key words.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Read and Interview:</b> In pairs, students receive two different short "announcements" or "mini-articles" for the newsletter (e.g., Announcement A: "Upcoming Book Fair - February 10th"; Announcement B: "Our New Science Project - Pictures Needed!"). Each student reads their own announcement. Then, they take turns "interviewing" their partner by asking questions about their text.
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Example Student A (after reading their text): "What is your article about?" Student B: "My article is about the new science project." Student A: "What else do you need for your article?" Student B: "I need pictures."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-style: italic;">Students use present, past, and future tenses as appropriate, and focus on emphasizing the main verb in future tense contractions (e.g., "I'll <b>collect</b> the photos").</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Reader's Corner" Contribution.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a short, previously unread story or report (2-3 paragraphs, A2.1 level) about a school event or a fictional class activity. The story will contain target vocabulary and grammar structures. Examples: "The Class Garden Project," "Our Amazing Field Trip to the National Park," or "The Mystery of the Missing Art Supplies."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Read and Discuss:</b> Groups read their assigned story silently and then discuss it to ensure everyone understands the main facts (Who, What, When, Where, Why). They should identify key events and details.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Formulate Questions:</b> Each group collaborates to write 3-4 comprehension questions about their story. These questions should target key information and use various tenses (present, past, future) as appropriate to the text. For instance, "When did the class go to the park?" (Past), "What will the students do next week?" (Future), "What is the main topic of the article?" (Present).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Write a Reflection:</b> After understanding the story, each group writes a short 1-2 sentence "reader's reflection" for the newsletter. This reflection should express their opinion or a key takeaway from the story. (e.g., "The story about the garden project was very interesting. We learned about teamwork!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Prepare for Sharing:</b> Groups prepare to read their story's title, their questions, and their reflection to the class. They will also state what kind of `pictures` they would `take` or `need` for their `article` if it were to be `published` in the newsletter.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> Students are actively engaging with a text (like a newsletter article) and preparing content that could be published, emphasizing the importance of clear information and reflective thoughts, and the type of `pictures` needed.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Group Work Rubric.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups present their story title, read their 3-4 comprehension questions, and share their written reflection. They also state what type of pictures they would need for the article. The teacher collects the written questions and reflections from each group.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (based on observation and written work):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Accuracy of answers to questions about the text (checked during observation and review of written questions).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Question Formation:</b> Correct use of present, past, and future simple in forming questions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary Use:</b> Incorporation of words like `article`, `newsletter`, `pictures`, `event`, `topic`, `teacher`, `student`, `school` in discussions and written reflections.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Reflection:</b> The reflection clearly conveys a thought or feeling about the story.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Demonstrated ability to distinguish short and long vowel sounds when orally stating words from the text (e.g., /ɑ/ in `art`, /eɪ/ in `take`).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participation and teamwork within the group.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket individually.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read a story and find the important facts. Yes / No"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a question about what I read. Yes / No"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My favorite part of the story was..."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn today about reading and understanding articles for our classroom newsletter?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about writing questions or reflections based on a text?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the reading activities worked well? Were students able to effectively identify information and formulate questions from the informational texts?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the texts appropriately leveled for A2.1/6th grade? Did students successfully integrate the target grammar (present simple questions, future, past simple) and vocabulary?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with comprehension or question formation for the next reading task? (e.g., providing question words, sentence frames). How can the phonemic awareness practice be reinforced?</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will verbally discuss their roles and responsibilities for the classroom newsletter, building on the content they read and analyzed today. They will share ideas for `articles` and `pictures` and practice using the present, past, and future tenses in conversation about `events` and `announcements`.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
</table>